Ladybird records local background images outside isolated groups in the
scroll node of their box. The box clips remain stationary, and the image
covers the scrollable overflow area. Compositor scrolling can therefore
move the background without recording another display list. Isolated
groups retain their offset-based recording path.

For local backgrounds, Blink permits scrolling-content painting in
LayoutBox::ComputeBackgroundPaintLocation(). BoxPainterBase clips images
to the inner border. The reduced scrolling case moves immediately under
wheel input in Chrome, and the positioning controls match their
content-background references.

WebKit’s BackgroundPainter::paintFillLayerImpl() offsets local image
geometry. Its RenderLayerBacking class requests repainting for local
images in needsRepaintOnCompositedScroll().

Gecko’s nsCSSRendering uses GetImageLayerClip() for local clipping, and
ComputeImageLayerPositioningArea() for background placement. Its
ScrollContainerFrame::ScrollToImpl() excludes local backgrounds from the
paint-skipping optimization.

The engines keep local image placement synchronized with scrolling
through different paint paths. Ladybird uses its scrolling visual
context for independently recorded layers, preserving its clip and each
background layer’s stacking order.
